                <pb facs="0735" n="697" sameAs="frus:frus1919Parisv11/pg697"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Paris Peace Conf. 184/250: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Commission to Negotiate
                        Peace</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-12-08T12:00:00-05:00">December 8,
                        1919—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received 10 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>3997. For Polk from the Secretary of State. Your telegram No. 5592 of December 6,
                    10 p.m. [<hi rendition="#italic">9 p.m.</hi>]. The President has agreed that
                    under the circumstances Ambassador Wallace may sit on the Supreme Council on
                    behalf of the United States as an observer but not as a participant. The
                    Ambassador is being instructed in this sense and he is also being instructed to
                    take no action and to express no opinion on any subjects which may be discussed
                    but to report the proceedings to Washington and to await instructions. The
                    Department is likewise giving to Ambassador Wallace full powers to sign the
                    Roumanian minorities treaty and the Hungarian treaty. Ambassadors Grey and
                    Jusserand have been informed of the above.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
